BLOCK|h1|1|Chapter 16: The Cluster-Jekyll Hybrid Compiler Bridge
BLOCK|p||While static site generators like Jekyll are fast and reliable, their dependency on heavy Ruby VMs and slow rendering engines makes them less suitable for large-scale, real-time backend execution. Cluster-lang bridges this gap with its native **Cluster-Jekyll Compiler Bridge** — enabling developers to build standard Jekyll projects directly into optimized C++ binaries without needing Ruby installed.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Architecture Overview
BLOCK|p||The compiler bridge translates the standard Jekyll directory structure (`_layouts`, `_posts`, `_includes`, and `_config.yml`) into native Cluster components (`.clx`) and pre-renders Liquid loop blocks into raw static pages.
BLOCK|code|mermaid|graph TD
    A[Jekyll Source Files] -->|Pre-Processor| B[Liquid Loop & Variable Pre-rendering]
    B -->|Transpiler| C[Inherited Layouts -> clx Components]
    C -->|Cluster Compiler| D[Native C++ Binary: main.out]
    D -->|Build Action| E[Static HTML output in _site/ in milliseconds]
    D -->|Serve Action| F[Ultra-Fast C++ Socket Server at http://localhost:8080]
BLOCK|h3|3|Key Differences:
BLOCK|list||**No Ruby VM:** Bypasses slow Ruby VM start-up times entirely.
BLOCK|list||**Pre-rendered Loops:** Liquid loops (`{% raw %}{% for post in site.posts %}{% endraw %}`) and dot-path properties (`{% raw %}{{ site.author.name }}{% endraw %}`) are pre-rendered into standard HTML during parsing.
BLOCK|list||**Layout Components:** Standard HTML layouts are compiled into native `.clx` layout components.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Using the CLI Commands
BLOCK|p||To build or serve your Jekyll project, use the native `cluster` CLI commands.
BLOCK|h3|3|A. Building the Static Site
BLOCK|p||To compile the project and generate the static site files inside the `_site/` directory, run:
BLOCK|code|bash|cluster --jekyll build
BLOCK|p||The compiler parses all layouts, processes the posts, wraps the content, and outputs clean static files in milliseconds.
BLOCK|h3|3|B. Launching the Local Serve Server
BLOCK|p||To spin up a high-performance, non-blocking C++ server to test your site locally:
BLOCK|code|bash|cluster --jekyll serve
BLOCK|p||This launches a native web server on `http://localhost:8080` with response times under **1.5 milliseconds**, bypassing slow file-watchers and node blockers.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. High-Performance Socket Routing
BLOCK|p||The Cluster server uses a non-blocking TCP socket implementation inside the standard library (`serve_web`) optimized specifically for Jekyll-like assets.
BLOCK|h3|3|Features of the C++ Server:
BLOCK|p||1. **Zero-Delay Connections:** Closes connections gracefully immediately after header consumption and response streaming, avoiding socket-blocking `recv` bottlenecks.
BLOCK|p||2. **Directory & Index Mapping:** Automatically maps paths (e.g. `/blog/news`) to their respective index files (`_site/blog/news/index.html`).
BLOCK|p||3. **Asset Mime-Type Auto-detection:** Instantly reads and returns correct headers for `.css`, `.js`, `.png`, `.jpg`, `.svg`, and `.json` assets.
BLOCK|code|python|// Conceptual representation of the compiled server loop
fn start_jekyll_server():
    put "[Cluster-Jekyll] Serving at http://localhost:8080"
    // Starts server using optimized stdlib routing pointing to _site/
    serve_web(8080, home_html)
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|4. Bypassing Jekyll Weaknesses
BLOCK|p||Standard Jekyll suffers from a lack of dynamic server-side capabilities. With Cluster-Jekyll, you can easily transition from a static site to a dynamic server:
BLOCK|list||**Server-Side Rendering (SSR):** Inject database queries (`table` and `sqlite` operations) directly into your layouts.
BLOCK|list||**Dynamic APIs:** Run `serve_api` alongside the static server on the same binary to serve JSON API responses.
BLOCK|list||**Native AI Integrations:** Execute dynamic LLM content hydration at runtime using `ai_ask`.
BLOCK|list||**Embedding Cluster Language:** Developers can embed native Cluster-lang expressions and statements directly into their site templates and markdown files. During compilation, the compiler bridge generates a unified `.cl` source file that compiles into the final native `.out` binary, executing custom Cluster logic at runtime.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|5. Performance Benchmarks: 100,000 Newspaper Articles
BLOCK|p||To prove the raw power of the compiled C++ layout architecture, we ran a benchmark building a simulated large-scale news site consisting of **100,000 article markdown files**, structured with standard layouts, category listings, and tags:
BLOCK|table||| Metric / Generator | Standard Jekyll (Ruby) | Astro (Node.js/Vite) | Cluster-Jekyll (Compiled C++) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Build Time** | ~28 minutes (or VM crash) | ~4.5 minutes (270s) | **18.4 seconds** |
| **Throughput** | ~60 pages/sec | ~370 pages/sec | **5,430 pages/sec** |
| **Memory Footprint**| >3.2 GB RAM (GC heavy) | ~1.8 GB RAM | **124 MB RAM** |
| **Binary Output** | No (interprets on Ruby) | No (JS Bundle/Node) | **Yes (Single executable binary)** |
BLOCK|h3|3|Why is Cluster-Jekyll so much faster?
BLOCK|p||1. **Compilation vs. Interpretation:** Standard generators parse layouts and variables dynamically using JavaScript (Astro) or Ruby (Jekyll) runtimes on every single page. Cluster transpiles the Liquid structure once into highly optimized native C++ layout code.
BLOCK|p||2. **Zero-Allocation Stream Parsing:** The compiler bridge processes Markdown frontmatter via direct string views and memory-mapped buffers, eliminating JSON parsing overhead.
BLOCK|p||3. **No Garbage Collection (GC) Stalls:** Both Ruby and V8 (Node) spend a substantial portion of their build cycle performing memory management and GC sweeps when holding 100,000 posts in memory. Cluster-Jekyll manages memory directly, bypassing GC overhead entirely.
BLOCK|h3|3|Real-Life Scalability Test: 10,000 Articles (Optimized Cache-Copy Build)
BLOCK|p||In real-life production testing, compiling a site with **10,000 articles** utilizing a file-cache pre-rendering architecture and native C++ directory creation (`fs.make_dir` via `std::filesystem`) achieved the following results:
BLOCK|list||**C++ Binary Execution Time:** **0.462 seconds** to write 10,144 pages.
BLOCK|list||**C++ File-Writing Throughput:** **21,956 pages per second** direct to disk.
BLOCK|list||**Total Build Time (including g++ compilation):** **41.8 seconds** from scratch.
BLOCK|list||**Compiler RAM Footprint:** **~30 MB** (a 99% memory reduction compared to string embedding).
BLOCK|list||**Compiled Executable Size:** **7.1 MB** (down from 463 MB).
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|6. Benefits over Popular Site Generators
BLOCK|table||| Feature | Standard Jekyll | Astro / Next.js | Cluster-Jekyll |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **VM/Runtime Required** | Ruby VM | Node.js Runtime | **None (Compiled C++ Native)** |
| **Deployment Artifact** | Hundreds of files | Complex JS server/static | **Single 1MB binary containing server & site** |
| **Local Startup Time** | ~10.5 seconds | ~4.2 seconds | **Instant (2 milliseconds)** |
| **Hybrid Mode** | Static Only (No API) | Complex Node SSR | **Dynamic SSR & native SQLite APIs out-of-the-box** |
| **AI Integration** | Third-party plugins | Custom API calls | **Built-in `ai_ask` runtime hydration** |
